A = {
"af181816": dict(
 bluf="Shaka turned the small Zulu chiefdom into southern Africa's most powerful kingdom through military reorganization and conquest. The wars and migrations of this era, the Mfecane, reshaped the region's peoples just before European settlers pushed inland.",
 why="Historians debate the causes: competition among chiefdoms for land, cattle and water during drought, and for trade at Delagoa Bay, combined with Shaka's military talent and ambition.",
 then="Peoples fled or were absorbed, and new states such as the Ndebele, the Sotho under Moshoeshoe and the Swazi took shape. Boer trekkers later found some areas depopulated and used that to justify claims to 'empty' land.",
 now="Shaka is a hero of Zulu identity, and the Zulu monarchy remains politically important in KwaZulu-Natal.",
 lens="Military reorganization can turn a minor polity into a regional power within a generation."),
"oc-musket": dict(
 bluf="The Musket Wars show how a new weapon spread through trade can turn old rivalries into a catastrophic arms race. The devastation helped lead some Māori chiefs to accept British protection in 1840.",
 lens="Arms races set off by uneven access to new weapons are among the deadliest dynamics in history."),
"sa-maratha1818": dict(
 bluf="The defeat of the Maratha confederacy removed the last Indian power able to challenge the East India Company. From 1818 the Company was the paramount power in India, ruling directly or through dependent princes.",
 why="Governor-General Hastings set out to eliminate rival Indian powers and end raiding by Pindari bands.",
 then="Much of India was now ruled through 'princely states' bound by treaty to the Company.",
 now="Over 500 princely states had to be absorbed into India and Pakistan in 1947, a legacy of this system; Nana Sahib, the last Peshwa's adopted heir, became a leader of the 1857 rebellion.",
 lens="Imperial conquest often ends by mopping up rivals already weakened by internal division."),
"as181926": dict(
 bluf="Raffles' free port gave Britain a strategic hub at the southern end of the Strait of Malacca. Free trade and open immigration made it a commercial magnet and laid the foundations of one of the world's richest city-states.",
 why="Britain wanted to counter Dutch control of the region's trade and secure a base on the route to China.",
 lens="Location plus openness can make a small port a global hub."),
"la181917": dict(
 bluf="Bolívar's victory at Boyacá freed New Granada (today's Colombia) from Spain and turned the tide of independence in northern South America. It launched campaigns that liberated five countries and created Gran Colombia, Bolívar's short-lived attempt at a great South American republic.",
 why="Spain's authority had collapsed after Napoleon invaded it in 1808, and Creole elites wanted self-rule. Spain's harsh reconquest after 1815 had turned many against it, and Bolívar needed a new strategy after repeated defeats in Venezuela.",
 then="He created Gran Colombia and helped free Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia. Independence was led by Creole elites, and the colonial social hierarchy, including the exclusion of Indigenous people, largely remained.",
 now="Bolívar is the 'Liberator' of five countries, and Hugo Chávez's 'Bolivarian revolution' and Venezuela's official name show his continuing political power.",
 lens="Wars of independence usually transfer power to local elites rather than overturn the social order."),
"na181931": dict(
 bluf="Florida's acquisition showed a pattern of US expansion: military pressure first, then a purchase from a weakened power. The same treaty fixed a border to the Pacific, giving the United States its first legal claim to the Pacific coast.",
 lens="Facts on the ground often come before the treaties that ratify them."),
"na182032": dict(
 bluf="The Missouri Compromise kept the Senate balanced between slave and free states by drawing a line across the West. It postponed but did not resolve the question of slavery's expansion, which eventually broke the Union.",
 lens="Compromises that divide territory instead of settling principles buy time but not peace."),
"eu182166": dict(
 bluf="Greece's war of independence was the first successful nationalist breakaway from the Ottoman Empire, won with the intervention of Britain, France and Russia. It set the pattern by which Balkan nationalism plus great-power interference dismantled Ottoman Europe over the next century.",
 lens="Small nationalist movements succeed when great powers decide it serves their interests."),
"la182118": dict(
 bluf="Mexico won independence not through Hidalgo's popular revolt but through a conservative deal that preserved the Church and elite privileges. It inherited a huge, divided territory and fragile institutions, and lost half of that territory to the United States within a generation.",
 why="Conservative Creole elites, alarmed by a liberal revolution in Spain that threatened Church and army privileges, decided independence was safer than rule from Madrid.",
 trig="Spain's liberal revolution of 1820, which restored its 1812 constitution.",
 then="Mexico's first decades brought constant coups and conflict between centralists and federalists, liberals and conservatives. Texas broke away in 1836, and the United States took the north in 1848.",
 now="Mexico became Latin America's largest Spanish-speaking nation, and arguments over the role of the Church, the army and the regions shaped its politics through the revolution of 1910.",
 lens="Independence won by conservatives tends to preserve the old social order, postponing conflicts rather than resolving them."),
"af182217": dict(
 bluf="Liberia was founded by an American society that combined anti-slavery sentiment with a wish to remove free Black people from the United States. Its Americo-Liberian settlers ruled over Indigenous Africans for over a century, a divide that fed the coups and civil wars of 1980-2003.",
 story="The American Colonization Society, which united some abolitionists with slaveholders who wanted free Black people out of the country, bought land on the West African coast and sent settlers from 1822. The newcomers, and Africans freed from slave ships, built a colony at Monrovia, often in conflict with the peoples already living there.",
 lens="Colonies founded as refuges can reproduce the hierarchies their founders fled."),
"la182219": dict(
 bluf="Brazil's independence under its own prince kept the country united and monarchical, avoiding the wars and fragmentation of Spanish America. It also preserved slavery and the plantation elite, making Brazil the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888.",
 lens="Smooth transitions preserve existing structures: good for stability, bad for those at the bottom."),
"na182333": dict(
 bluf="The Monroe Doctrine declared the Western Hemisphere closed to new European colonization, staking a US claim to a regional sphere of influence. Unenforceable at first, it became the justification for a century of US interventions in Latin America.",
 why="Latin American nations had just won independence, and the United States feared European monarchies might help Spain reconquer them, or that Russia would expand down the Pacific coast.",
 then="Theodore Roosevelt's 1904 'corollary' claimed a right to intervene in Latin American countries, used to justify occupations of Haiti, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.",
 now="It is still the basis of US policy that treats the Americas as its sphere of influence, and US officials have revived its language in response to Chinese and Russian activity in the region.",
 lens="Great powers claim spheres of influence near home; watch how they treat their neighbours' sovereignty."),
"la182420": dict(
 bluf="Ayacucho destroyed Spain's last major army in South America and ended its mainland American empire; Spain kept only Cuba and Puerto Rico.",
 lens="Empires rarely end with one battle: Ayacucho was the last act of a collapse that began 15 years earlier in Madrid."),
"se-burma1824": dict(
 bluf="The First Anglo-Burmese War began Britain's conquest of Burma, which took 60 years and three wars. It brought Assam and coastal Burma under British rule and set the colonial border between Burma and India.",
 lens="Expanding empires collide at their frontiers, and border clashes become wars of conquest."),
"eu182567": dict(
 bluf="The Stockton and Darlington Railway proved that steam locomotives could haul freight and passengers commercially. Railways slashed transport costs and became the backbone of industrial economies and of national integration.",
 lens="Infrastructure that slashes transport costs reshapes where people live and work."),
"jp-repel": dict(
 bluf="The order to fire on any foreign ship was the high point of Tokugawa isolation policy. Its failure to deter Western ships showed that isolation could no longer be enforced.",
 lens="Rigid policies often peak just before they collapse."),
"na182534": dict(
 bluf="The Erie Canal linked the Great Lakes to the Atlantic through New York and cut freight costs by around 90%. It made New York City America's commercial capital and tied the Midwest's economy to the Northeast rather than the South.",
 lens="Transport links shape political alliances: the canal bound the Midwest to the North before the Civil War."),
"se-javawar": dict(
 bluf="The Java War was the last great resistance by Java's aristocracy to Dutch rule, and its cost pushed the Dutch to impose the exploitative Cultivation System. Diponegoro became a national hero of Indonesian independence.",
 lens="The cost of crushing a revolt is often passed on to the colonized through harsher extraction."),
"d-decembrist": dict(
 bluf="The Decembrist revolt was the first attempt by Russian elites to force constitutional government. Its failure led Nicholas I to rule as a strict autocrat, and the Decembrists became martyrs for later Russian revolutionaries.",
 lens="Failed elite revolts often bring tighter autocracy, pushing opposition underground and toward radicalism."),
"af183018": dict(
 bluf="France's conquest of Algeria began its largest settler colony and 132 years of colonial rule. Algeria was made legally part of France and settled by about a million Europeans, which made its eventual war of independence one of the bloodiest of decolonization.",
 why="A diplomatic insult provided the pretext; Charles X wanted military prestige to shore up his unpopular regime.",
 then="Conquest took decades against Emir Abd al-Qadir and others, with massacres and land confiscations. Algeria was divided into French departments, and European settlers took the best land.",
 lens="Settler colonies are the hardest to decolonize, because the settlers become a political force of their own."),
"la183021": dict(
 bluf="The collapse of Gran Colombia ended Bolívar's vision of a large, unified South American republic. Latin America fragmented into many states dominated by regional elites and military strongmen (caudillos).",
 lens="Large states assembled in war often fragment in peace, when regional elites no longer need each other."),
"na183035": dict(
 bluf="The Indian Removal Act authorized the forced relocation of Native nations from the Southeast to lands west of the Mississippi. It made dispossession federal policy, defied the Supreme Court's recognition of tribal sovereignty, and is now widely described as ethnic cleansing.",
 why="Settlers wanted Native land for cotton and, in Georgia, gold, and Jackson and the Southern states rejected tribal sovereignty.",
 then="About 60,000 Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw and Seminole people were removed in the 1830s, and thousands died on the journeys, most notoriously on the Cherokee 'Trail of Tears' (1838). The Seminole resisted removal in Florida for years.",
 now="It is now widely described as ethnic cleansing and underlies today's debates over Native sovereignty.",
 lens="When economic interest and political power align against a minority, legal protections alone rarely save it."),
"se-cultivation": dict(
 bluf="The Cultivation System forced Javanese villages to grow export crops for the Dutch state, making Java enormously profitable for the Netherlands. It is a classic case of a colony financing its colonizer, at the cost of famine in parts of Java.",
 lens="Colonial profit often came from forced production, not trade."),
"eu183068": dict(
 bluf="The July Revolution overthrew the restored Bourbon monarchy and replaced it with a liberal constitutional king. It showed the Vienna settlement could not freeze Europe and inspired revolts in Belgium, Poland and Italy.",
 lens="Attempts to roll back political gains often provoke the revolution they were meant to prevent."),
"eu183068b": dict(
 bluf="Belgium's revolt broke up the United Kingdom of the Netherlands created at Vienna and produced a new state whose neutrality the great powers guaranteed. That guarantee brought Britain into war in 1914.",
 lens="Guarantees of a small state's neutrality can become triggers for great-power war."),
"d-liverpool": dict(
 bluf="The Liverpool and Manchester Railway was the first modern intercity line, with timetabled steam trains carrying both passengers and freight. It proved railways' commercial potential and set off a global railway boom.",
 lens="One successful demonstration can trigger an investment boom."),
"d-baptistwar": dict(
 bluf="The Baptist War, the largest slave uprising in the British Caribbean, convinced Parliament that slavery could not be maintained without constant violence. It hastened the 1833 Abolition Act.",
 lens="Resistance by the oppressed changes the calculations of distant lawmakers."),
"d-beagle": dict(
 bluf="Darwin's voyage gave him the observations (fossils, geology, island species) that led to his theory of evolution by natural selection. It also shows how imperial survey voyages produced scientific knowledge.",
 lens="Breakthroughs often come from comparing variation across places."),
"d-worcester": dict(
 bluf="Worcester v. Georgia recognized Native nations as distinct political communities over which states had no authority. Jackson's refusal to enforce it showed that court rulings are only as strong as the executive's willingness to carry them out.",
 lens="Courts have no armies: their rulings depend on others to enforce them."),
"eu183369": dict(
 bluf="Britain's abolition of slavery freed about 800,000 people across its empire and turned the world's leading power into an opponent of slavery. It compensated the slave owners, not the enslaved, and kept freed people in years of unpaid 'apprenticeship'.",
 then="Protests ended apprenticeship early, in 1838, and Caribbean sugar declined. Britain used its navy and diplomacy to press other countries to end slavery, while the owners' compensation was invested in Britain.",
 now="British taxpayers finished repaying the loan for that compensation only in 2015, and Caribbean governments cite this history in their demands for reparations.",
 lens="Abolition can be negotiated to protect those who profited: who gets compensated shows where power lies."),
"d-factoryact": dict(
 bluf="The 1833 Factory Act began state regulation of industrial working conditions, banning young children from mills and creating inspectors to enforce it. It marked a shift: the state would intervene in markets to protect the vulnerable.",
 lens="Regulation means little without enforcement; inspectors made this law different."),
"af183519": dict(
 bluf="The Great Trek took thousands of Boers out of British rule into the interior, where they fought African kingdoms and founded independent republics. It became the founding myth of Afrikaner nationalism and set up the conflicts that led to the Boer War and apartheid.",
 why="Anger at British rule: the abolition of slavery (1834) with what Boers saw as inadequate compensation, Anglicization, and British frontier policy toward the Xhosa.",
 then="They founded the Transvaal and Orange Free State republics.",
 now="The Trek is central to Afrikaner nationalism and to the roots of apartheid.",
 lens="Groups that leave to escape a state's reforms often build societies that entrench what was reformed."),
"d-alamo": dict(
 bluf="The Alamo became the rallying cry of Texan independence, which led to US annexation and the Mexican-American War. It remains a contested symbol: heroic sacrifice in Texan memory, but part of a revolt partly about preserving slavery.",
 lens="Defeats can mobilize more than victories when they become martyrdom stories."),
"na183836": dict(
 bluf="The forced removal of the Cherokee, despite their victory at the Supreme Court, became the defining symbol of Native dispossession. Around a quarter of those removed died.",
 lens="Legal victories cannot protect a group that lacks political power."),
"oc-myall": dict(
 bluf="Myall Creek was the first time colonial authorities hanged white men for massacring Aboriginal people. The backlash among colonists helped ensure later frontier killings went unpunished and unrecorded.",
 lens="Prosecuting atrocities can provoke a backlash that makes future accountability harder."),
"as183927": dict(
 bluf="Britain went to war to protect its opium trade and force China open to Western commerce. China's defeat ended its ability to set the terms of its foreign relations and began the 'century of humiliation' that drives Chinese nationalism today.",
 story="The East India Company grew opium in India and smuggled it into China to pay for tea. By the 1830s millions of Chinese were addicted and silver was flowing out. In 1839 Commissioner Lin Zexu seized and destroyed about 20,000 chests of opium at Canton. Britain sent warships that outgunned Chinese forces along the coast and up the Yangtze.",
 why="Britain bought huge amounts of tea while China wanted few British goods, and Britain closed the gap with opium. China tried to suppress the drug trade, and Britain also demanded diplomatic equality and open trade.",
 trig="Lin Zexu's destruction of British opium at Canton in 1839.",
 then="The Treaty of Nanjing (1842) ceded Hong Kong, opened five ports and imposed an indemnity; later treaties exempted foreigners from Chinese law. Opium imports kept growing, and a Second Opium War (1856-60) forced further concessions.",
 now="China calls this the start of its 'century of humiliation', a theme in its politics today.",
 lens="Trade imbalances can turn into wars when one side uses force to fix them."),
"as183928": dict(
 bluf="The Tanzimat reforms tried to save the Ottoman Empire by modernizing its army, law and administration along European lines and promising equality to all subjects. They strengthened the central state but could not stop nationalist breakaways or European interference.",
 lens="Defensive modernization can strengthen a state but also raise expectations it cannot meet."),
"af184020": dict(
 bluf="Sultan Said's move to Zanzibar made it the hub of an East African commercial empire built on cloves, ivory and enslaved people. The caravan trade it organized reached deep into central Africa and opened the routes later used by European explorers and colonizers.",
 lens="Commercial empires follow profit: the sultan moved his capital to where the money was."),
"oc-waitangi": dict(
 bluf="The Treaty of Waitangi was New Zealand's founding agreement between the British Crown and Māori chiefs, but its English and Māori texts said different things about sovereignty. That gap justified British rule and land seizures, and since 1975 has been the basis for redress, making the treaty central to New Zealand politics.",
 then="Settler demand for land led to the New Zealand Wars (1845-72) and huge confiscations; by 1900 Māori held only a small fraction of their land.",
 lens="When a treaty's two versions disagree, the stronger party's reading prevails, until the weaker party gains the power to contest it."),
"as184229": dict(
 bluf="The Treaty of Nanjing was the first of the 'unequal treaties' that forced China to open ports, cede territory and grant foreigners privileges. It set the pattern of Western domination that lasted a century.",
 then="France and the United States demanded similar treaties, and 'most-favoured nation' clauses meant any concession to one power went to all.",
 now="Hong Kong's return in 1997 and the 'century of humiliation' narrative make Nanjing a lasting symbol in Chinese politics.",
 lens="Treaties signed under duress breed nationalism that later demands their reversal."),
}
